[Event "Basic tactics: the fork, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/sdDSE6ge"]
[FEN "8/5q1p/8/5NK1/6P1/3k4/8/8 b - - 11 52"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5q1p/8/5NK1/6P1/3k4/8/8_b_-_-_11_52"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ You are White, and he is a queen for a knight ahead. We will now learn the fork: one move, two targets, and he can only save one. }
52... Ke4 { His king walks out, and now both red squares are a single knight hop from the same spot. Land there and you hit his king and his queen at once. [%csl Re4,Rf7][%cal Yf5d6] } 53. Nd6+ { Check, and every legal move he has left is a king move. While he is saving his king, his queen gets no chance to run. [%cal Gd6e4,Gd6f7] } (53. Nh6 { Your knight attacks his queen, but there is no check, so she simply strolls away and you have won nothing at all. } 53... Qe7+ 54. Kh5 { She left with a check of her own, and now his queen hunts your king along the edge and forces mate. }) (53. Ne7 { Your knight lands right beside his queen with nothing guarding it, so she eats it for free. } 53... Qxe7+ 54. Kh6 { Your knight is gone, one lonely pawn is all you have left, and his queen mates your king in three. }) 53... Kd5 { His king runs, and his queen is still sitting on exactly the square your knight attacks. Go and take her. [%cal Yd6f7] } 54. Nxf7 { His queen is yours and nothing can take your knight back. One knight, two targets, and he could save only one: that is a fork. } (54. Nf5 { Your knight scuttles home and lets his queen off the hook, and both targets vanish in a single move. } 54... Ke5 55. Nh4 Qf4 { His queen checks you and starts hunting your king. A knight and a pawn cannot hold her off, and he has a forced mate. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: fork practice one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/uL3WWVrr"]
[FEN "8/8/5kp1/5n1p/4K3/8/8/6R1 w - - 2 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/5kp1/5n1p/4K3/8/8/6R1_w_-_-_2_58"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ You are Black, with a knight and two pawns against his rook. Watch his rook move, then go hunting for a fork. }
58. Rh1 { His rook drops back into the corner, far away from his own king. Where can your knight jump to attack two things at once? } 58... Ng3+ { Check, and the rook in the corner is attacked as well. He has to answer the check, so that rook is not allowed to run. [%cal Gg3e4,Gg3h1] } (58... h4 { You push the pawn and let the fork slip away, because with no check on the board his rook is free to walk off. } 59. Rg1 Ne7 60. Kf4 { His rook has slipped away from your knight and nothing of his is loose. The position is dead level. }) (58... Nd6+ { A check, yes, but look at what else that knight touches: his rook is nowhere near any of it. } 59. Kf3 { His king simply steps aside and you have gained nothing. A check with only one target is just a check. }) 59. Kf4 { Every move he had was a king step, and no piece of his can come and guard that corner. Go and collect the rook. } 59... Nxh1 { A whole rook, and you gave nothing for it. That is a fork: two targets at once, and he could only rescue one. } (59... Ne2+ { You rescue your knight and forget the rook, and a fork you never collect was worth nothing at all. } 60. Ke3 Ng3 61. Ra1 { His rook has left the corner and your knight has nothing left to bite on. Neither side can make progress. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: fork practice two"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/io8m2o9v"]
[FEN "5q1k/1Q1nN3/4pn1p/3p2p1/1p1PB3/rPb1P1BP/5PP1/5RK1 b - - 0 32"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5q1k/1Q1nN3/4pn1p/3p2p1/1p1PB3/rPb1P1BP/5PP1/5RK1_b_-_-_0_32"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ A busy board this time. He is about to grab a bishop and he feels perfectly safe, which is exactly when a fork turns up. }
32... dxe4 { His pawn snaps up your bishop, so he is a knight up for a pawn. Now your knight has one jump that attacks two men at once. } 33. Ng6+ { Check, and his queen right behind the king is attacked as well. He has to move his king, so she cannot get away. [%cal Gg6h8,Gg6f8] } (33. Nc6 { Your knight hops out to a safe square, but it attacks nothing he would mind losing. } 33... Qa8 34. Qc7 { His queen has strolled to safety, and you are left a knight down for one pawn. }) (33. Be5 { You attack his knight, but he has a much bigger capture waiting and he takes first. } 33... Qxe7 34. Bxf6 Qxf6 { He is a bishop and a knight up for one pawn, and the fork never happened. }) 33... Kg8 { His king steps aside, because a check has to be answered first. His queen is still sitting where your knight can take her. } 34. Nxf8 { His queen comes off the board for your knight, and that is a bargain. One jump, two targets: the fork did it again. } (34. Ne5 { Your knight backs off and his queen is free again, so the fork you found is handed straight back. } 34... Qa8 35. Qc7 { You had his queen trapped and let her walk. He keeps his extra knight and you keep nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: the pin, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/qmwSksav"]
[FEN "r1b2rk1/3n1pp1/pq2p2p/1pb5/4B2B/5N2/PPPQ2PP/2KR1R2 w - - 1 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2rk1/3n1pp1/pq2p2p/1pb5/4B2B/5N2/PPPQ2PP/2KR1R2_w_-_-_1_16"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ You are Black, and we will now learn the pin. A pinned piece is stuck: if it steps aside, something bigger behind it is taken. }
16. Bxa8 { He grabs your rook, so he is a rook up for a pawn. Now look: his queen and his king sit on one diagonal with nothing in between. [%csl Rd2,Rc1][%cal Yc5e3] } 16... Be3 { Your bishop pins his queen against his king. She cannot step aside now, because that would leave his king in check. [%cal Ge3d2,Gd2c1] } (16... Qa7 { You tuck your queen away safely and let the pin go, and that pin was the only thing this position had. } 17. Qc3 { Nothing at all has happened. He is still a whole rook up for one pawn, and his pieces are perfectly comfortable. }) (16... a5 { A quiet pawn push while you are a rook down, and it hands him a free move to sort his king out. } 17. Rfe1 b4 18. Kb1 { His king has stepped off that diagonal, so there is nothing left to pin and he keeps your rook. }) 17. Qxe3 { He takes your bishop with his queen, which was the only square she was still allowed to visit. [%cal Yb6e3] } 17... Qxe3+ { Your queen takes his, with check. You paid a rook and a bishop, he paid his queen, and you come out ahead. } (17... Qc7 { You forget to take his queen back, and your bishop has simply been given away. } 18. Bg3 { Your bishop is gone for nothing. He is a rook and a bishop up, and only one pawn went the other way. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: pin practice one"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/qFpAnWmi"]
[FEN "2kr2nr/ppp3pp/4N1b1/3p4/8/4P2q/PPP1B3/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 3 18"]
