[Role "exercise"]

{ You are White and the material is close. His queen is about to grab something, and a pin can turn the whole game around. }
18... Qxe6 { His queen eats your knight, so he is two pawns ahead. Now ask yourself where his king is standing. } 19. Bg4 { Your bishop pins his queen to his king: she is standing on the line between them and cannot step off it. [%cal Gg4e6,Ge6c8] } (19. Bd2 { A quiet developing move, and the pin is gone the moment his king can step away. } 19... Nf6 20. Qe1 { He is two pawns up, his pieces are coming out, and the chance you had has quietly gone. }) (19. Rf4 { Your rook swings across, but it attacks nothing and it traps nothing at all. } 19... Kb8 20. Bd2 { You have gained nothing, he is still two pawns up, and his king has walked out of the pin. }) 19... Qxg4+ { His queen is stuck on that line, so she takes your bishop, and it arrives with a check you have to answer. } 20. Qxg4+ { Your queen takes his, with check. A queen for a bishop, and you have gone from two pawns behind to a winning game. } (20. Kh2 { You step out of the check and let his queen live, which throws away everything the pin had won. } 20... Qh4+ 21. Kg2 { His queen and bishop are hunting your king now and he has a forced mate. You gave a bishop and got nothing back. }) 1-0


[Event "Basic tactics: the skewer, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/hnHmSl7U"]
[FEN "6k1/5pp1/5bn1/r2B3Q/2pP4/2P3P1/5P2/6K1 w - - 2 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6k1/5pp1/5bn1/r2B3Q/2pP4/2P3P1/5P2/6K1_w_-_-_2_41"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ You are Black, and we will now learn the skewer. Check his king, and then take whatever was hiding on the line behind him. }
41. Qh1 { His queen tucks in right behind his king, and both of them now sit on the first row where your rook can reach. [%csl Rg1,Rh1][%cal Ya5a1] } 41... Ra1+ { Check along the first row, and he has nothing but king moves. Whichever way he steps, his queen is what your rook runs into. [%cal Ga1g1,Ga1h1] } (41... Bxd4 { You grab a pawn with the bishop while his queen sits there for the taking, and she is worth far more. } 42. Qe4 Rxd5 43. Qxd5 { His queen slipped away and ate your rook on the way. A queen against your bishop and knight is losing for you. }) (41... Ra7 { Your rook steps back to safety and the skewer goes with it, because nothing is checking his king any more. } 42. Bxc4 { He helps himself to a pawn, his queen is comfortable, and you are simply worse off than before. }) 42. Kh2 { His king steps up beside his queen. Now run your rook along the row and take her. [%cal Ya1h1] } 42... Rxh1+ { You trade a rook for his queen: his king takes back, and you are left a knight up for a pawn. In a skewer the big piece stands in front. } (42... Ra7 { You retreat instead of collecting, and his queen strolls out of the corner alive. } 43. Qb1 { The skewer has gone for good and his queen is safe. He is clearly better and you have nothing to show. }) 0-1


