[Role "exercise"]

{ He is about to snatch a pawn and the position looks like a perfectly ordinary middlegame. It is not: there is a skewer here. }
22... Bxg5 { His bishop helps itself to your pawn, so he is a pawn ahead. Now look hard at where his king and his queen are standing. } 23. Qh8+ { Check straight along his back row. He has nothing but king moves, and behind him on that row sits his queen. [%cal Gh8e8,Ge8a8] } (23. Nb3 { A sensible knight move, but the check was the whole point and the moment goes with it. } 23... Bxc1 24. Nxc1 Nd8 { He kept your pawn and his queen sits safe in the corner. The skewer has gone and the game is level. }) (23. Bd3 { You tidy your pieces up and hand him a completely free move to look after his king. } 23... Ke7 { His king has stepped off the row and there is nothing behind him any more, so the skewer is gone. }) 23... Ke7 { His king steps off the row to escape the check, and his queen is left in the corner with nothing guarding her. } 24. Qxa8 { A whole queen for nothing, collected from the far corner. That is the skewer: check the king, then take what was behind him. } (24. Qh3 { You bring your queen home instead of collecting the piece she has just won. } 24... Nd8 25. Qg4 { His queen is safe, his extra pawn still counts, and you are the one who is slightly worse. }) 1-0


