{ Almost nothing is left: his knight and four pawns against your bishop and four pawns. Watch where his knight goes. }
33. Nd1 { His knight drops back to the edge, and nothing of his is defending it now. Your bishop has been quiet all game. } 33... Bh5+ { Check from the far side of the board, and his knight is sitting on the same line just behind him. [%cal Gh5f3,Gf3d1] } (33... Kd6 { A quiet king step, and his knight is loose at this very moment: a chance like that does not come twice. } 34. Ne3 Ke6 35. Kg3 { His knight has jumped to a guarded square. Knight against bishop with the pawns locked is a dead draw. }) (33... Bg6 { Your bishop steps out, but it arrives without a check and gives him all the time he needs. } 34. Kg3 { From there your bishop does not even look at his knight, so his free move costs him nothing at all. }) 34. Kg2 { His king steps aside, because every single move he had was a king move. Now look all the way along that diagonal. } 34... Bxd1 { A whole knight for nothing, taken from the far side of the board. That is the skewer: check first, then take what was behind. } (34... Bg6 { You tuck your bishop away and let his knight walk out of the trap for free. } 35. Kh3 Kd6 36. Nf2 { His knight has skipped to safety and neither side can break through the locked pawns. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: the discovered attack, lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/Wo1dJhaW"]
[FEN "8/pp4k1/3r2pp/4N3/3n3P/6P1/P2R2PK/8 w - - 10 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/pp4k1/3r2pp/4N3/3n3P/6P1/P2R2PK/8_w_-_-_10_41"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ You are Black, and we will now learn the discovered attack: one of your pieces steps out of the way, and the piece behind it strikes. }
41. Nf3 { His knight offers a trade. Notice that your own knight stands between your rook and his, blocking the column. [%csl Gd6,Rd2][%cal Yd4f3] } 41... Nxf3+ { Your knight leaves the column with check and grabs a defended knight, and the rook behind it wakes up. [%cal Gd6d2] } (41... Nf5 { You rescue your knight, but it never opened the column, so the rook behind it still has nothing to attack. } 42. Rb2 b6 43. Rc2 { His rook has walked off the column and the material is dead level. There is nothing left here to play for. }) (41... h5 { A pawn move at the moment your knight was the whole story, and it hands him a free capture. } 42. Nxd4 { He simply takes your knight and the discovered attack has gone. You are a knight down for nothing. }) 42. gxf3 { He has to deal with the check before anything else, and that is the whole trick: his rook gets no help. [%cal Yd6d2] } 42... Rxd2+ { A free rook, and with check. The discovered attack bought you the one move he did not have time to spend. } (42... Ra6 { Your rook swings away from the column just as it was about to matter, and his rook is safe. } 43. Rd7+ Kf6 44. Rxb7 { His rook checked you and then ate your pawn. He is the one collecting now and the game has levelled out. }) 0-1


