{ Your rook and his rook face each other down one column, with your own bishop in between. Making that bishop move is the whole puzzle. }
21. h3 { He makes a little air for his king and pays no attention to that column. Your bishop needs to leave with a bang. } 21... Bh2+ { Your bishop jumps out of the column with check, and it offers itself to his king at the same time. [%cal Gd8d1] } (21... c5 { A pawn push while the two rooks stare at each other, and the bishop between them never moves. } 22. Nc3 cxb4 23. axb4 { The pawns came off, the material is dead even, and the discovered attack has quietly vanished. }) (21... Be7 { Your bishop does step off the column, but without a check he has all the time he wants. } 22. Nd4 { His knight has dropped into the column between the two rooks, and now your rook sees nothing at all. }) 22. Kxh2 { He grabs the free bishop, and that was his whole move. His rook has been sitting undefended the whole time. } 22... Rxd1 { His rook is yours and you gave a bishop for it. That is a discovered attack: the check bought you the move you needed. } (22... Ra8 { You forget to take his rook, so the bishop you just gave away has bought you absolutely nothing. } 23. Nd4 c6 24. Nb3 { You are a whole bishop down with nothing to show, and his rook is not even being attacked any more. }) 0-1


[Event "Basic tactics: the back rank mate, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/oLCBkefa/4UUeXlma"]
[FEN "7k/1p4pp/p1b5/8/6N1/7P/PP1R1K2/4r3 b - - 4 38"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7k/1p4pp/p1b5/8/6N1/7P/PP1R1K2/4r3_b_-_-_4_38"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ You are White, and we will now learn the back rank mate. A king with its own pawns in front of it has no air to escape into. }
38... Rb1 { His king sits on the last row and its own two pawns block the way out. Send your rook all the way to that row. [%csl Rh8,Rg7,Rh7][%cal Yd2d8] } 39. Rd8+ { Check, and his king has no square at all to run to. Throwing a piece in the way is his only legal move. [%cal Gd8h8] } (39. Ne5 { A fine square for your knight, but mate was already sitting on the board and this quietly lets it go. } 39... g6 40. Nxc6 bxc6 { You traded pieces off and his pawn step gave his king a breathing hole. Dead level now, with no mate anywhere. }) (39. h4 { You make air for your own king and hand him the free move he was desperate for. } 39... h6 { His pawn has stepped up and given his king a square of its own, so the mate has gone. }) 39... Be8 { He throws his bishop in the way, which is all a blocker ever does. Take it. [%cal Yd8e8] } 40. Rxe8# { Mate! The two pawns he never moved are what trapped his king, and your rook owns the last row: that is the back rank mate. } (40. b3 { You had mate in one and pushed a pawn instead, which hands him exactly the move he needed. } 40... Rb2+ 41. Kf3 { His rook is chasing your king around while he gets time to open a window. The mate you had is gone. }) 1-0


