{ His king is boxed in on the last row again, but this time one of his own pieces is guarding the door. }
19... cxd6 { He takes your rook and is a bishop and a pawn ahead. His queen is the only thing covering the last row beside his king. [%csl Rh8,Rg7,Rh7] } 20. Qf8+ { Your queen steps right in beside his king. Nothing but his queen can take her, and she cannot be ignored either. [%cal Gf3f8] } (20. Rf2 { You defend instead of striking, and the mate that was on the board disappears. } 20... Be6 21. Qxb7 Bg8 { He is a bishop up, his pieces are safe, and the mate you walked past is long gone. }) (20. h3 { A quiet pawn move, and the whole idea evaporates while he gets organised. } 20... Bd7 21. Qxb7 Bc6 { He is a bishop up and his bishop now stares at the squares round your king. There is nothing left of your attack. }) 20... Qxf8 { His queen has to take, it is his only legal move, and she lands on the very square she was guarding. } 21. Rxf8# { Mate! You gave your queen so his guard had to take her, then your rook took the guard. A back rank mate, bought with a queen. } (21. Rf2 { You forget the mate and hand his queen a free rook, with check into the bargain. } 21... Qxf2+ 22. Kxf2 { A rook, a bishop and a pawn down, instead of the mate you had in one. Both queens are off the board. }) 1-0


