{ You are White, and we will now learn to remove the defender. Take the guard off the board first, then take the prize. }
28... Rc8 { His rook lands on your column, and his queen is the only piece defending it. [%csl Rc8][%cal Ge6c8] } 29. Qxe6 { Take the guard first, even though it costs you a queen for a queen. His rook is what you are really after. [%cal Yc4e6] } (29. axb4 { You grab a pawn, and his rook drops onto your queen instead. } 29... Rxc4 30. dxc4 axb4 { His rook ate your queen and you only won a rook back. A queen against your rook is simply lost. }) (29. Rc2 { You defend your queen, and he takes her anyway because a rook is a cheap price for a queen. } 29... Rxc4 30. dxc4 { Your queen is gone for his rook, and his queen alone is stronger than your rook and pawns. }) 29... fxe6 { He takes back with the pawn. The guard is off the board and his rook stands there with nobody to help it. [%cal Yc1c8] } 30. Rxc8+ { A whole rook, with check, and it cost you nothing more. Remove the defender first, then collect the prize. } (30. Rb1 { You forget the rook that your queen was spent to win, and he gets a completely free move. } 30... bxa3 31. Kxa3 { Level again, and the queen you spent bought you nothing at all. }) 1-0


